  <h1>India’s Climate Diplomacy at COP28 — Balancing Development and Responsibility</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>At COP28 (Dubai, 2023) and follow-ups in 2024, India reaffirmed its climate commitments while emphasizing the principle of “common but differentiated responsibilities” (CBDR). The Hindu highlighted how India seeks to balance its developmental needs with global climate leadership. For UPSC, this is a key issue linking environment, economy, and foreign policy.
________________________________________
India’s Climate Commitments
1.	Net-Zero Target: 2070.
2.	Non-Fossil Capacity: 500 GW by 2030.
3.	Emission Intensity Reduction: 45% reduction by 2030 (from 2005 levels).
4.	Green Energy Share: 50% of electricity from renewables by 2030.
________________________________________
Key Outcomes at COP28
•	Global Stocktake: Reviewed progress since Paris Agreement.
•	India’s Position:
o	Advocated phase-down (not phase-out) of coal.
o	Called for greater climate finance from developed countries ($100 billion+ unmet pledge).
o	Highlighted initiatives like International Solar Alliance (ISA) and LiFE (Lifestyle for Environment).
________________________________________
Balancing Development and Responsibility
•	Energy Security: Coal still ~70% of electricity mix; immediate phase-out unrealistic.
•	Equity Argument: Developed nations have historical responsibility for emissions.
•	Poverty & Growth: India needs affordable energy to sustain growth and welfare.
•	Renewable Push: India added record solar capacity but faces grid and storage challenges.
________________________________________
Challenges
1.	Finance Gap: Requires $10 trillion by 2070 for net-zero pathway.
2.	Technology Dependence: Needs access to advanced green tech (storage, CCUS).
3.	Just Transition: Coal-state livelihoods (Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh) at risk.
4.	Adaptation Needs: Extreme weather events (floods, heatwaves) rising.
________________________________________
Strategic Significance
•	Global Leadership: India projects itself as voice of Global South.
•	Diplomacy: Builds credibility in forums like G20 and BRICS+.
•	Soft Power: Initiatives like ISA showcase India as solution-provider.
•	Domestic Gains: Green jobs, technology investment, and reduced pollution.
________________________________________
UPSC Relevance
•	GS-III (Environment): Climate change, renewable energy, sustainable development.
•	GS-II (IR): India’s role in global negotiations, South–South cooperation.
•	Essay:
o	“Sustainable development is the pathway to the future we want.” (Ban Ki-moon)
o	“Climate justice is inseparable from development justice.”
•	PSIR Paper II: International regimes and North–South divide.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Climate Finance Mobilization: Push developed nations for predictable, concessional finance.<br/>2.	Technology Partnerships: Collaborate with EU, US, Japan for green tech transfer.<br/>3.	Domestic Policy Integration: Align energy, transport, and urban planning with NDCs.<br/>4.	Community Resilience: Invest in climate adaptation — coastal protection, drought management.<br/>5.	Just Transition Plans: Reskill coal workers and diversify coal-belt economies.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s climate diplomacy reflects a pragmatic balancing act — asserting equity while embracing ambitious green targets. Success will depend on mobilizing finance, technology, and inclusive domestic policies. For UPSC aspirants, this issue demonstrates how environment, diplomacy, and development converge in India’s global strategy.</p>
